    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d977">

                <pb facs="1169" n="1159" sameAs="frus:frus1943v01/pg1159"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.42/3<unclear/>: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-12-16T13:00:00+01:00">December 16,
                            1943—1 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 16—9:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8732. Chinese observer at Education Conference (Embassy’s telegram No. 8556,
                    December 9) asked Embassy observer yesterday what <pb facs="1170" n="1160"
                        xml:id="pg1160"/> Embassy proposed to do about Foreign Office invitation to
                    join Conference. When told that invitation was forwarded to Department, he asked
                    if Embassy had received any indication that United States might accept
                    invitation without making effort to obtain changes of nature suggested in Turner
                    memorandum submitted with Embassy’s despatch No. 12183 of November 11.<anchor
                        n="12" xml:id="d977-f1"/> He added that his Government is prepared to join
                    Conference on basis outlined by Turner during conversations with Chinese
                    Ambassador but displayed obvious concern about possibility that China might be
                    left in isolated position as sole supporter of such a basis. In order to keep
                    situation fluid and to assure Chinese cooperation should Department wish to
                    press Turner recommendations or similar proposals, Embassy observer offered to
                    inform Chinese observer of any indications he might receive to the effect that
                    United States might accept invitation without pressing for changes in make up
                    Conference. This offer was received gratefully and it is believed that Chinese
                    will not take action on their invitation until our policy is determined.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d977-f1" n="12" xml:id="d977-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; Mr. Turner made recommendations for transforming the Conference into
                    a United Nations Educational and Cultural Conference with an inclusive
                    international program in the educational and cultural fields (800.42/306).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
